\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-6}} \\\\ y &={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.18$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/2f/2f56bfb6777e6d95dfd887a525ea69a58725fc4f1a81bea84ebde67a72812518.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/2c/2c9158f2818c1630debba65361b9be35a583d74591e389728528ffc3409ed89b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478412 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a4095b6-ac86-5129-b614-5959c7d2c664" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478412 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =2,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward $\\text{infinity}$ in the phase plane as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t{e}^{2t} \\\\ y &={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.18$ | $0$ | $1.36$ |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $1$ | $2.72$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/2f/2f56bfb6777e6d95dfd887a525ea69a58725fc4f1a81bea84ebde67a72812518.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/2c/2c9158f2818c1630debba65361b9be35a583d74591e389728528ffc3409ed89b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478476 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186478490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478476 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4a094494-860c-5096-883d-aa966a9b908d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478476 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186167214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478488 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b69b8d43-1d59-555c-896c-7caa0cb68dcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478488 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186478412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478490 #edb/ref